I've spent many a sunless day and moonless night in the dark caverns beneath
those fair woods I once called home, and in that time, I've learned of the
wonder and the horror that lies in the wizard's dungeon.  Few things are more
curious, however, than the tale of Andrigo Sipaliwini, a squat, fierce human
whose shop is at the end of ten stairways leading down.  I was in dire need
of food, and Sipaliwini had an ample supply, but, as is the unfortunate habit
of shopkeepers, he was unwilling to part with his waybread and slime molds
without compensation.  I was recently robbed by a fair nymph and a leprechaun,
obviously in league with each other, and so I had nothing with which to pay
Andrigo.  I did, however, have a wand of polymorph.  Recalling the dire 
and vague warnings about killing a shopkeeper, I decided that I should
polymorph him instead, and then run while he chased me as a lesser creature.
I aimed my wand and squeezed it lightly, focusing my inner strength, and the
wand glowed.  Suddenly, Sipaliwini the shopkeeper became Sipaliwini the grey
unicorn!  He was obviously upset with me, and, being a tourist of mild manner,
I didn't want to kill such a unique beast.  I drew a useless gem (I know not
what kind) from the floor of the shop and hurled it at Sipaliwini.  He was
further maddened, but disappeared into thin air.  Surprisingly, when I found
him again, he was calm and composed, and every time I threw a gem at him, he
because furious, then neighed and disappeared, and soon forgave me.  When I
last saw Andrigo, he had returned to his shop and was adjusting to his new
form well.  If, someday, fate claims this body, I hope to adjust to the 
change as well as he.

